\title{Layer Selection Sensitivity in Hidden-State Safety Probes for Reasoning Models}

\author{Anonymous ARR Submission}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
\input{sections/abstract}
\end{abstract}

\input{sections/introduction}

\input{sections/related_work}

\input{sections/method}

\input{sections/experiments}

\input{sections/discussion}

\input{sections/conclusion}

\input{sections/limitations}

\bibliography{references}

\input{sections/appendix}

\end{document}

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}

Reasoning models such as DeepSeek-R1~\cite{deepseekr1} and QwQ~\cite{qwq} scale inference-time computation through extended chains of thought (CoT). Recent work documents that these models can ``self-jailbreak'': without adversarial input modification, the CoT transitions from safety-aware deliberation to committed harmful generation~\cite{harmthoughts,selfjailbreak}. We define the first reasoning step after which safety deliberation permanently ceases as the \textbf{Safety Commitment Point} (CP). This failure mode differs qualitatively from adversarial jailbreaks on standard LLMs because the harmful transition originates within the model's own reasoning. Detecting this transition in real time is essential for monitoring systems that must intervene before harmful content reaches users. While production-grade monitoring remains an open challenge, systematic characterization of what current methods can and cannot detect is a necessary first step.

Existing safety detectors target fundamentally different problems. Hidden-state (HS) methods such as TrajGuard~\cite{trajguard} and SafeSwitch~\cite{safeswitch} classify adversarial inputs at the token level, while text monitors like SafeRemind~\cite{saferemind} scan generated reasoning for explicit violations. Neither tracks the gradual, multi-step commitment process. A fundamental question remains: should a monitoring system read the model's hidden states or its generated text? This comparison has never been conducted under controlled conditions. Na\"ive reasoning suggests HS features (up to 5120 dimensions) should dominate text embeddings (384d sentence encoder), but no prior work tests this systematically. A further complication is that HS probes require selecting which transformer layer to monitor, a choice rarely scrutinized but potentially decisive.

We present the first systematic evaluation across four reasoning models spanning two architectures (Llama, Qwen) and two scales (7--8B, 32B). Our central finding is methodological: the choice of layer selection metric entirely determines whether an HS advantage is observed. A composite metric (balanced accuracy $\times$ crossing rate) selects shallow layers for 32B models because shallow representations are highly compressed (inter-step cosine distances 1.4--2.7$\times$ smaller than at deep layers), so a linear probe operates near the noise floor and threshold crossings reflect noise rather than semantic transitions. At these layers, only 5/12 statistical tests survive Holm-Bonferroni correction. Metrics from two complementary measurement families converge on deep layers (\cref{tab:layer_selection}), where HS probes significantly outperform text probes on all four models, each on three correlated metrics (Holm-Bonferroni corrected, \cref{sec:evaluation}; precision +15.4 to +32.5pp; \cref{tab:hs_vs_text}).

Our contributions are:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Layer selection sensitivity.} We identify a systematic bias in composite metrics that reverses HS probe conclusions at 32B scale. A five-metric ablation establishes principled selection criteria (\cref{sec:layer-results}).

    \item \textbf{Encoder-conditional HS advantage at corrected layers.} Against MiniLM-384d, HS probes significantly outperform text on all four models, each on three correlated metrics (Holm-Bonferroni corrected; precision +15.4 to +32.5pp). The advantage persists after PCA dimensionality matching (10/12 tests at 384d) but diminishes against BGE-large-1024d (1/4 models significant). At 7--8B scale, evidence is strong; at 32B, model-specific caveats apply: QwQ-32B's position baseline achieves higher balanced accuracy (0.832 vs.\ HS 0.796), though HS probes retain higher precision (+4.5pp) and lower FPR ($-$3.0pp), with negative HS lead time (\cref{sec:32b-analysis}).

    \item \textbf{Negative results define method boundaries.} No alternative method consistently matches supervised probing across all four models (\cref{tab:negative}): BOCPD/CUSUM fail on gradual transitions; Mahalanobis distance finds near-zero separation; RepE achieves 0.756 balanced accuracy on QwQ-32B (comparable to supervised 0.778) but at substantially lower precision (0.512 vs.\ 0.700), illustrating the metric-dependent nature of method comparison; cross-model transfer fails under PCA alignment. Control task selectivity (12.5--19.7pp above chance; \cref{app:control}) confirms genuine safety structure.
\end{itemize}

