\section{Related Work}\label{sec:related-work}

\paragraph{Safety in Reasoning Models.}
Reasoning models introduce a qualitatively new failure mode: the model's own chain of thought can transition from safety-aware deliberation to harmful generation without adversarial input modification~\cite{wang2025lrmsafety}. \citet{harmthoughts} document this self-jailbreak phenomenon with step-level annotations, while \citet{selfjailbreak} show that longer reasoning chains increase circumvention probability. \citet{hagendorff2026lrm_jailbreak} demonstrate that reasoning models can autonomously jailbreak other LLMs with 97\% success, identifying reasoning capability itself as a safety threat. H-CoT~\cite{hcot2025} shows reasoning chains can be externally hijacked, complementing internally-originating transitions. Unfaithful reasoning~\cite{chen2025unfaithful} further motivates hidden-state monitoring that bypasses text. On mitigation, SafeThink~\cite{safethink} steers early reasoning toward safety, SafeRemind~\cite{saferemind} inserts entropy-triggered reminders, and \citet{korbak2025cot_monitorability} argue that CoT monitorability is valuable but fragile. \citet{mao2025selfjailbreak_reasoning} further characterize self-jailbreak in reasoning models and propose Chain-of-Guardrails as a defense. \citet{baker2025monitoring} show that CoT monitoring can detect reward hacking in reasoning models but warn that optimization pressure promotes obfuscation, complementing our focus on passive detection. \citet{yin2025refusal_cliff} discover that safety-aligned reasoning models maintain refusal intentions during thinking but experience a sharp drop at output generation, a phenomenon our temporal probes could detect. \citet{ahrend2026safer_traces} address information leakage in traces; \citet{hu2026refusal_dynamics} trace refusal dynamics in standard LLMs. None conducts a systematic modality comparison for commitment detection.

\paragraph{Hidden-State Safety Detection.}
Internal activations can detect safety violations in standard LLMs. \citet{zhou2024alignment} show safety-relevant information distributes across layers, while \citet{qi2025shallow_alignment} find that alignment operates primarily over early output tokens. \citet{arditi2024refusal} identify a single refusal direction, but \citet{zhao2025harmfulness} show harmfulness and refusal are encoded separately. \citet{joad2026refusal_multi} reveal eleven category-specific refusal directions, and \citet{wollschlager2025geometry} formalize multi-dimensionality through concept cones, supporting our finding that single-direction methods are insufficient. TrajGuard~\cite{trajguard} uses Mahalanobis distance from safe trajectories; SafeSwitch~\cite{safeswitch} trains hidden-state classifiers; SIREN~\cite{siren} monitors internal representations during generation; SafeDream~\cite{safedream2026} proposes proactive world models for detection. These methods assume threats from external input manipulation and operate at the token level on instruction-tuned models. The self-jailbreak setting differs because transitions are internal and span multiple reasoning steps. \citet{kadali2025internal} decompose internal layer representations to separate jailbreak from benign prompts, confirming that layer-wise structure carries safety-relevant signal. We reproduce TrajGuard (\cref{sec:negative}) and find its geometric approach fails because discriminative signal resides in minor PCA components.

\paragraph{Probing Methodology and Layer Selection.}
Linear probing has a long history from layer-wise classifiers~\cite{alain2017understanding} through sentence probing~\cite{conneau2018probing} to comprehensive surveys~\cite{belinkov2019analysis,belinkov2022probing}. \citet{hewitt2019control} introduce control tasks to distinguish structure from memorization, which we adopt (\cref{app:control}). \citet{belrose2023leace} establish theoretical limits on linear probe extraction; \citet{elazar2021amnesic} test whether probed information is causally used. \citet{tenney2019bert} show syntactic information in middle layers and semantics in deeper layers. \citet{zhang2025probing_selfverify} apply hidden-state probing to self-verification in reasoning models, providing a complementary perspective on what reasoning-model representations encode. Concurrent work by \citet{alvarez2026transport_geometry} uses hidden-state geometry for step-level hallucination detection, sharing our temporal probing framework but targeting a different phenomenon. \citet{boxo2025linear_probes} show that linear probes can rely on textual evidence rather than deeper representations, motivating our comparison of hidden-state probes against text-based monitors. Despite this foundation, layer selection remains underexamined: most studies select by a single metric without sensitivity analysis. We demonstrate that this practice can reverse a study's central conclusion (\cref{sec:layer-results}).

\paragraph{Change-Point Detection.}
BOCPD~\cite{adams2007bayesian} detects abrupt distributional shifts; CCS~\cite{burns2023discovering} discovers latent truth directions via consistency constraints; RepE~\cite{zou2023representation} identifies concept directions through mean differences. We adapt all three to temporal commitment detection (\cref{sec:negative}). BOCPD and CUSUM fail because commitment is gradual. CCS provides no gain because its consistency objective misaligns with temporal structure. RepE shows inconsistent performance across models (0.756 on QwQ, 0.559 on R1-32B), motivating supervised probes with principled layer selection as the minimal effective methodology.

