\section{Problem Setup and Methods}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Safety Commitment Point}
\label{sec:commitment-point}

When a reasoning model is jailbroken, its chain-of-thought (CoT) transitions from safety-aware deliberation to committed harmful generation. We define the \textbf{Safety Commitment Point} (CP) as the first reasoning step after which safety reasoning permanently ceases. Steps before CP are \emph{pre-commitment} ($y_t = 0$); steps at or after CP are \emph{post-commitment} ($y_t = 1$). The detection task produces a score $s_t \in [0, 1]$ estimating whether the model has passed its CP by step $t$.

We study four reasoning models spanning two architectures and two scales (\cref{tab:models}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Arch.} & \textbf{Params} & \textbf{Layers} & \textbf{Hidden} & \textbf{$n$ (total\,/\,test)} \\
\midrule
R1-8B & Llama & 8B & 32 & 4096 & 209\,/\,43 \\
OT-7B & Qwen & 7B & 28 & 3584 & 228\,/\,47 \\
R1-32B & Qwen & 32B & 64 & 5120 & 100\,/\,20 \\
QwQ-32B & Qwen & 32B & 64 & 5120 & 95\,/\,19 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Models studied. R1-8B and R1-32B are distilled from DeepSeek-R1~\citep{deepseekr1}; QwQ-32B~\citep{qwq} is a native reasoning model; OT-7B~\citep{openthinker} is trained with STILL~\citep{still} on Qwen2.5-7B. Data: HarmThoughts~\citep{harmthoughts} with step-level CP annotations. Split: 60/20/20.}
\label{tab:models}
\end{table}

\subsection{Probe Architecture}
\label{sec:probe-architecture}

\paragraph{Hidden-state (HS) probes.} At each step $t$, we extract the last-token hidden state $\mathbf{h}_t^{(\ell)} \in \mathbb{R}^d$ from layer $\ell$ and train an L2-regularized logistic regression classifier (balanced class weights, $C{=}1.0$, LBFGS, max\_iter${}=2000$) on binary CP labels. Layer selection is analyzed in \cref{sec:layer-sensitivity}.

\paragraph{Text probes.} Each step's text is encoded with all-MiniLM-L6-v2 ($\mathbb{R}^{384}$). The same logistic regression is applied, providing a surface-level modality baseline.

\paragraph{Temporal windowing.} For window size $W$, we concatenate features from steps $[t{-}W{+}1, \ldots, t]$. We evaluate $W \in \{1, 3, 5, 10, 15, 20, 25\}$ and select per-model on validation. GRU sequence models are also evaluated; for fair comparison, \cref{tab:gru_multi_seeds} reports GRU results under the same 60/20/20 split used for all LR experiments (\cref{app:gru_details}).

\subsection{Layer Selection Sensitivity}
\label{sec:layer-sensitivity}

Layer selection is a researcher degree of freedom that can reverse modality comparison conclusions. A composite metric (balanced accuracy $\times$ crossing rate) systematically selects shallow layers for 32B models: L2 (3\% depth) for QwQ-32B, L10 (16\%) for R1-32B. Shallow representations are highly compressed (inter-step cosine distances 1.4--2.7$\times$ smaller than at deep layers), so a linear probe operates near the noise floor and threshold crossings are noise-driven (\cref{app:cosine_volatility}).

A five-metric ablation reveals that metrics from two complementary measurement families (accuracy-based and false-positive-based) converge on deep layers: L60 (94\%) for QwQ-32B, L63 (98\%) for R1-32B. At these layers, all 12 Holm-Bonferroni tests pass (\cref{sec:evaluation}); at biased layers, only 5/12 survive. Full per-layer results appear in \cref{app:layer_sweep}.

\subsection{Baseline Methods}
\label{sec:baselines}

We evaluate seven alternatives to contextualize probe performance (\cref{tab:negative}): BOCPD~\citep{adams2007bayesian} and CUSUM for change-point detection; Mahalanobis distance following TrajGuard~\citep{trajguard}; RepE mean-difference directions~\citep{zou2023representation}; two-layer MLP probes; CCS-adapted~\citep{burns2023discovering}; and cross-model transfer via PCA. A position-only baseline ($t/T$) controls for temporal artifacts. Detailed configurations in \cref{app:bocpd_config,app:transfer_details}.

\subsection{Evaluation Protocol}
\label{sec:evaluation}

We report balanced accuracy, precision, pre-CP FPR (step-level and trace-level), coverage, and lead time (steps between first detection and true CP). Detection requires $K{=}5$ consecutive threshold crossings, selected as the Pareto-optimal point balancing coverage and false positive rate (\cref{app:k_ablation}). Modality comparisons use two-sided null-centered bootstrap (10{,}000 resamples) with Holm-Bonferroni step-down correction (3 metrics $\times$ 4 models $= 12$ tests, $\alpha{=}0.05$). Within each model, the three metrics are positively correlated by construction, making the correction stricter than under independence. Control task selectivity~\citep{hewitt2019control} validates genuine representational structure (\cref{app:control}).

\section{Experiments}\label{sec:experiments}

We evaluate temporal probes across four reasoning models (\cref{tab:models}), comparing hidden-state (HS) and text modalities under different layer selection criteria.

\subsection{Setup}\label{sec:setup}

We study R1-8B (Llama-8B, R1-distilled, $n{=}43$ test traces), OT-7B (Qwen-7B, STILL-trained, $n{=}47$), QwQ-32B (Qwen-32B, native reasoning, $n{=}19$--$20$), and R1-32B (Qwen-32B, R1-distilled, $n{=}20$). All traces come from HarmThoughts with step-level CP annotations (60/20/20 split). HS probes extract last-token hidden states from a selected layer; text probes encode each step with all-MiniLM-L6-v2 (384d). Both use L2-regularized logistic regression with temporal windowing $W \in \{1, 3, 5, 10, 15, 20, 25\}$ selected on validation. Detection requires $K{=}5$ consecutive threshold crossings. Statistical testing follows \cref{sec:evaluation}.

\subsection{Layer Selection Results}\label{sec:layer-results}

\cref{tab:layer_selection} shows that the biased composite metric (balanced accuracy $\times$ crossing rate) systematically selects shallow layers for 32B models: L2 (3\% depth) for QwQ-32B and L10 (16\%) for R1-32B. Metrics from two complementary measurement families (accuracy-based: pure balanced accuracy, additive composite; false-positive-based: precision, inverse FPR) converge on deep layers: L60 (94\%) and L63 (98\%). The mechanism is that shallow layers produce highly compressed representations with small inter-step margins: step-to-step cosine distances are 1.4--2.7$\times$ smaller than at deep layers across all four models (\cref{app:cosine_volatility}). A linear probe in this compressed space operates near the noise floor, generating frequent threshold crossings that inflate crossing rate independently of discriminative power (\cref{sec:layer-sensitivity}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Biased}$^a$ & \textbf{Corrected}$^b$ & $p$ \textbf{(corr.)} \\
\midrule
R1-8B & L14 (44\%) & L14 (44\%) & ${<}0.001$ \\
OT-7B & L0 (0\%) & L16 (57\%) & ${<}0.001$ \\
QwQ-32B & L2 (3\%) & L60 (94\%) & ${<}0.005$ \\
R1-32B & L10 (16\%) & L63 (98\%) & ${<}0.001$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Layer selection sensitivity. $^a$\,bal\_acc $\times$ crossing\_rate. $^b$\,Consensus of metrics from two measurement families. Rightmost: HS vs.\ text precision $p$-value at corrected layer. At biased layers, 32B $p$-values are 0.064 (R1-32B) and 0.35--0.83 (QwQ), both non-significant.}
\label{tab:layer_selection}
\end{table}

A per-layer metric heatmap (\cref{fig:layer_heatmap}) visualizes the systematic divergence between biased and corrected layer selections across all four models. The downstream impact is severe: at biased layers, only 5 of 12 tests survive Holm-Bonferroni correction (all from 7--8B models unaffected by the bias). At corrected layers, all 12 pass (Holm-Bonferroni, \cref{sec:evaluation}). Had we reported only biased-layer results, we would have concluded a scale-dependent HS advantage that is entirely a methodological artifact.

\subsection{HS vs.\ Text Comparison}\label{sec:hs-text}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{llclrrlrc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Layer} & \textbf{Enc.} & $W$ & $\Delta$\textbf{BA} & $\Delta$\textbf{Prec} & \textbf{95\% CI} & $\Delta$\textbf{FPR} & \textbf{HB} \\
\midrule
R1-8B & L14 & 384d & 15 & +8.2 & \textbf{+32.5} & [+20.2, +45.2] & $-$7.4 & 3/3 \\
 & & 1024d & & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & +5.8 & [$-$3.0, +15.0] & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & 0/1 \\[2pt]
OT-7B & L16 & 384d & 3 & +10.7 & +21.9 & [+14.4, +29.6] & $-$17.1 & 3/3 \\
 & & 1024d & & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & +18.1 & [+9.0, +27.0] & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & 1/1 \\[2pt]
QwQ & L60 & 384d & 3 & +15.4 & +23.6 & [+12.9, +35.1] & $-$9.8 & 3/3 \\
 & & 1024d & & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & +9.4 & [$-$3.0, +22.0] & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & 0/1 \\[2pt]
R1-32B & L63 & 384d & 1 & \textbf{+18.7} & +15.4 & [+8.7, +22.6] & \textbf{$-$14.6} & 3/3 \\
 & & 1024d & & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & +11.7 & [+1.0, +22.0] & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & 0/1 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{HS vs.\ text at corrected layers (LR probes, 60/20/20 split). Enc.: text encoder (384d = MiniLM-L6-v2, 1024d = BGE-large). $\Delta$: HS$-$text difference (pp). 95\% CI: bootstrap interval for $\Delta$Prec (10{,}000 resamples). 384d rows: HB over 12 tests (4 models $\times$ 3 metrics, $\alpha{=}0.05$); 1024d rows: HB over 4 tests (precision only). BGE $\Delta$BA/$\Delta$FPR not computed.}
\label{tab:hs_vs_text}
\end{table}

Against the 384d baseline, HS probes significantly outperform text probes on all four models across all three metrics (\cref{tab:hs_vs_text}). At 7--8B, the evidence is strong: precision gains of +32.5pp (R1-8B) and +21.9pp (OT-7B), stable across seeds (std ${<}4$pp). At 32B, all tests pass Holm-Bonferroni but with model-specific caveats: R1-32B achieves significance only at $W{=}1$ with 25\% coverage, and QwQ-32B's position baseline is competitive (\cref{sec:32b-analysis}). Control task selectivity confirms genuine safety signal: 12.5--19.7pp above chance (\cref{app:control}).

\paragraph{Dimensionality control.} PCA reduction of HS features to 384d (matching text encoder dimensionality, 79--92\% variance retained) preserves the precision advantage on all four models (+16.8 to +23.1pp, all $p{<}0.012$), with 10/12 Holm-Bonferroni tests passing. The two non-significant comparisons are R1-8B balanced accuracy (+1.3pp, $p{=}0.731$, effect too small to detect at $n{=}43$) and QwQ-32B FPR ($-$11.4pp favoring HS, $p{=}0.038$, below the Holm-Bonferroni threshold). All four precision comparisons pass. This confirms a modality effect rather than a capacity artifact.

\paragraph{Encoder robustness.} The 1024d rows in \cref{tab:hs_vs_text} replace MiniLM (384d) with BGE-large (1024d, a 2.7$\times$ stronger encoder). The HS precision advantage persists directionally on all four models but narrows substantially: only OT-7B survives Holm-Bonferroni (1/4 tests). The advantage is encoder-conditional rather than absolute. Layer selection (Contribution 1) is encoder-independent, as it concerns hidden-state properties across layers rather than the text baseline.

\paragraph{Caveats.} R1-32B achieves significance only at $W{=}1$ with 25\% coverage (5/20 traces); at $W{\geq}3$, dimensionality ($51200 \times W$ for $n{=}20$) prevents significance. Sweeping regularization $C$ over $[0.01, 100]$ yields ${<}2$pp variation in balanced accuracy, confirming robustness to hyperparameter choice. QwQ-32B HS lead time is negative ($-$1.2 to +1.9 steps vs.\ text 3.7--12.3 steps): higher precision at later detection. On benign traces, probes produce 39--52\% false alarm rates, indicating that current methods characterize the commitment phenomenon rather than constitute production-ready detectors. Per-model details in \cref{sec:32b-analysis}.

\subsection{32B Model-Specific Analysis}\label{sec:32b-analysis}

R1-32B and QwQ-32B share the Qwen-32B architecture (64 layers, 5120 hidden) but present opposite detection profiles (\cref{tab:32b_divergence}), making training method the likely differentiator.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Cov.} & \textbf{Lead (steps)} & \textbf{HB} & \textbf{Pos.\ margin} \\
\midrule
R1-32B & 25\% & +11.7 to +13.3 & 3/3 ($W{=}1$) & +14.2pp \\
QwQ-32B & 74\% & $-$1.2 to +1.9 & 3/3 (all $W$) & $-$3.6pp \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{32B cross-model divergence. R1-32B provides early warning with narrow coverage; QwQ-32B provides robust significance with poor lead time. Pos.\ margin: HS balanced accuracy minus position-only baseline.}
\label{tab:32b_divergence}
\end{table}

R1-32B detects commitment 11--13 steps early with a +14.2pp margin over the position baseline, confirming genuine representational signal, but covers only 25\% of traces at the single significant window ($W{=}1$). QwQ-32B achieves statistical significance at all window sizes with 74\% coverage, but HS probes fire later than text probes (negative lead time). The position baseline achieves higher balanced accuracy than HS probes (0.832 vs.\ 0.796) because harmful steps tend to occur later in traces; however, HS probes achieve higher precision (+4.5pp: 0.564 vs.\ 0.519) and lower FPR ($-$3.0pp: 0.217 vs.\ 0.247), indicating that hidden states capture safety-specific patterns beyond positional information. The divergence implies that per-model validation is essential: no single detection configuration generalizes even within the same architecture at the same scale, and a system validated on one 32B model would fail on the other.

\subsection{Temporal Context}\label{sec:temporal}

Temporal windowing ($W{>}1$) improves both modalities on all four models (\cref{fig:window_ablation}). Text precision improves +8 to +23pp from $W{=}1$ to $W{=}25$; HS precision improves +1.7 to +12.9pp. At 7--8B, the modality advantage (+22 to +33pp precision) exceeds the temporal improvement (+13pp). Both effects are complementary: the most robust deployment default is temporal windowing ($W{=}3$--$15$) combined with principled layer selection. Full per-model temporal ablations appear in \cref{app:temporal_details}.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_temporal_improvement.pdf}
\caption{Temporal window effect on R1-8B HS probe (L14). Precision: 69.3\% ($W{=}1$) to 82.2\% ($W{=}25$); FPR: 9.65\% to 4.8\%.}
\label{fig:window_ablation}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Negative Results}\label{sec:negative}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{p{1.5cm}p{2.9cm}p{2.3cm}}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{Result} & \textbf{Limitation} \\
\midrule
BOCPD & 0\% det.\ on 3/4 models & Commitment gradual \\
CUSUM & $<$ threshold, all models & Same as BOCPD \\
Mahalanobis & $\Delta d \approx 0$ & Signal in minor PCs \\
RepE & QwQ 0.756; R1-32B 0.559$^a$ & Model-dependent \\
MLP & 3/4 worse than LR & Overfitting \\
CCS & No gain over supervised$^b$ & Alignment mismatch \\
Transfer & Best 59.5\% (PCA)$^c$ & Model-specific repr. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Seven alternative approaches. $^a$\,RepE QwQ approaches supervised bal\_acc but precision is 0.512 vs.\ LR 0.700. $^b$\,Semi-supervised (requires CP labels). $^c$\,Best direct transfer 67.3\% but 1\% crossing rate.}
\label{tab:negative}
\end{table}

No alternative method consistently matches supervised probing across all four models (\cref{tab:negative}). We prioritize precision over balanced accuracy as the primary evaluation criterion because false positives on pre-CP steps waste intervention resources (triggering unnecessary safety responses), while missed post-CP steps merely reduce coverage without causing immediate harm. This framing is critical for interpreting RepE results below.

\paragraph{Change-point methods.} BOCPD~\citep{adams2007bayesian} achieves 0\% detection on 3/4 models because safety commitment is gradual rather than abrupt: the probe score sequence transitions smoothly over 5--15 steps without the discontinuity that BOCPD's hazard function requires. On R1-8B (the sole model with non-zero detection), the best BOCPD configuration ($\lambda{=}10$) achieves 93\% detection rate but with lower lead time (+3.5 steps) than simple thresholding (+4.9 steps). CUSUM similarly fails because cumulative evidence never exceeds the decision boundary on smooth transitions. The gradual nature of commitment is itself an empirical finding: it rules out the entire class of abrupt-change detectors and motivates the supervised probe approach that explicitly learns the transition boundary.

\paragraph{RepE inconsistency.} RepE mean-difference directions~\citep{zou2023representation} illustrate the metric-dependent nature of method comparison. On QwQ-32B, RepE achieves 0.756 balanced accuracy (comparable to supervised LR at 0.778), but its precision is 0.512 vs.\ LR 0.700 and its FPR is 0.309 vs.\ 0.120. The mean-difference direction captures enough signal to classify steps above chance, but fires indiscriminately on pre-CP steps, producing 2.6$\times$ more false alarms than supervised probes. On R1-32B, RepE nearly fails entirely (0.559 balanced accuracy). The inconsistency across two models sharing the same architecture confirms that a single linear direction computed from class means does not reliably capture safety commitment structure.

Cross-model transfer produces no practical utility across 17 configurations; stronger alignment methods (CCA, model stitching~\cite{kornblith2019cka,bansal2021stitching}) remain unexplored. Detailed per-method analysis in \cref{app:negative_details}.

