\section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Layer selection as researcher degree of freedom.}
Layer selection is an underexamined methodological choice in the probing literature that can silently determine a study's conclusions. Our five-metric ablation (\cref{sec:layer-results}) demonstrates that a single metric component, the crossing rate, reverses the central finding of an HS vs.\ text comparison: at biased layers, only 5 of 12 tests survive Holm-Bonferroni correction, producing an apparent scale-dependent modality effect; at corrected layers, all 12 Holm-Bonferroni tests pass (\cref{sec:evaluation}). Step-to-step cosine distance analysis (\cref{app:cosine_volatility}) reveals the mechanism: shallow representations are highly compressed, with inter-step cosine distances 1.4--2.7$\times$ smaller than at deep layers. A linear probe in this compressed space operates near the noise floor, so threshold crossings are noise-driven rather than reflecting genuine semantic transitions. Any composite metric that incorporates crossing rate is susceptible to this bias. This bias is not specific to safety probing. We hypothesize that any threshold-based detection study that uses crossing rate as a layer selection criterion, whether for sentiment, factuality, or toxicity, could produce analogous reversals. Validation on other probing tasks is needed to confirm generality.

\paragraph{Recommendations for probing methodology.} We recommend that probing studies report results under at least two complementary layer selection criteria, and that five-metric ablations (or at minimum, results at multiple candidate layers spanning shallow, middle, and deep positions) become standard practice. Even among unbiased metrics, the choice between threshold-based and threshold-free criteria affects layer selection: AUROC tends to select layers at shallower depth (3--50\%) than threshold-dependent metrics (44--98\%), further demonstrating that layer selection methodology is a critical researcher degree of freedom. At AUROC-optimal layers, HS probes retain directional precision advantage on all four models (+6.2 to +23.0pp), though these layers yield lower absolute performance than the deep layers selected by threshold-dependent metrics. The cost of ablation is modest: evaluating five metrics across all layers adds minutes to an experiment that takes hours. The alternative is that conclusions about representational structure reflect layer selection methodology rather than the phenomenon under study. The control task provides independent corroboration: R1-32B selectivity increases from 7.5pp at the biased layer (L10) to 19.7pp at the corrected layer (L63), confirming that the biased metric selected layers where positional features dominate over genuine safety structure.

\paragraph{Temporal context and modality choice.}
Information-theoretic reasoning suggests that hidden states (4096 to 5120 dimensions of internal computation) should dominate text features (384-dimensional sentence embeddings) purely by signal capacity. Both temporal context and modality choice significantly improve detection, but their relative magnitudes differ by scale. On R1-8B, the modality advantage (precision +32.5pp at $W{=}1$) exceeds the temporal improvement (+12.9pp from $W{=}1$ to $W{=}25$), establishing that modality choice matters more than temporal context at this scale. At 32B, the relative magnitudes depend on model and window size (\cref{sec:temporal}). Text probes benefit more from temporal context than HS probes because they start from a weaker single-step baseline and extract proportionally more from observing multi-step progressions. Safety commitment is a gradual process: the model progressively consolidates its intent over multiple reasoning steps rather than switching abruptly at a single token. Both modalities benefit from temporal context; for text probes, the improvement is universal across all four models. The practical implication is that temporal windowing ($W{=}3$ to $15$) remains the most robust single default for safety monitoring, as it provides guaranteed improvement regardless of modality, while the modality choice requires principled layer selection to realize its full potential.

\paragraph{Cross-model divergence within identical architecture.}
R1-32B and QwQ-32B share the same base architecture but present opposite detection profiles (\cref{tab:32b_divergence}): R1-32B provides early warning (+11--13 steps) with narrow coverage (25\%), while QwQ-32B provides broad coverage (74\%) with negative lead time. Training method (R1 distillation vs.\ native reasoning) is the likely differentiator, since architecture and scale are held constant. Per-model validation is essential: a system validated on one 32B model would fail on the other.

\paragraph{Design implications for safety monitoring.}
We recommend temporal windowing ($W{=}3$--$15$) combined with principled layer selection (balanced accuracy or precision, never crossing rate) as deployment defaults. At 7--8B, HS probes Pareto-dominate text probes (+32.5pp precision on R1-8B, +21.9pp on OT-7B). At 32B, practical trade-offs require model-specific decisions between lead time, coverage, and significance breadth (\cref{sec:32b-analysis}). All methods produce high safe-trace FPR (39--52\%), possibly because safe traces contain commitment-like state transitions (e.g., the model deciding to refuse) that probes cannot distinguish from harmful commitment. The 85.5\% oracle gap (\cref{sec:oracle}) confirms that passive monitoring captures a small portion of available signal; hidden states encode the model's current safety state but carry limited predictive power about future trajectory evolution.

