\section{Limitations}\label{sec:limitations}

\paragraph{32B model-specific caveats.}
R1-32B achieves significance only at $W{=}1$ (25\% coverage, 5/20 traces); dimensionality ($51200 \times W$ for $n{=}20$) prevents larger windows. QwQ-32B's position baseline achieves higher balanced accuracy (0.832 vs.\ HS 0.796) and HS lead time is negative, suggesting positional rather than representational signal. Multi-seed analysis (\cref{app:multi_seed}) confirms scale-dependent robustness: 7--8B precision std ${<}8$pp across 5 seeds, while R1-32B std $= 12.8$pp. This motivates a two-tier framing: 7--8B results are robust; 32B results are pilot-scale with caveats.

\paragraph{High false positive rate and oracle gap.}
Safe-trace FPR is 39--52\% at standard thresholds, so current probes characterize the phenomenon rather than constitute production detectors. The 85.5\% oracle gap confirms that passive probing captures only a fraction of available signal.

\paragraph{Statistical methodology.}
Layer, $W$, and threshold are selected on the same validation set ($n{=}4$--$9$ at 32B); $C$-insensitivity (${<}2$pp) partially mitigates but does not eliminate overfitting. Bootstrap CIs at $n{=}19$--$20$ may exhibit below-nominal coverage~\citep{efron1993bootstrap}; $p$-value resolution with 10{,}000 resamples is $10^{-4}$.

\paragraph{Scope.}
We cover 2 architectures and 2 scales (7--8B, 32B); intermediate scales (13B, 70B) and proprietary reasoning models (o1, o3) are missing. The text baseline uses MiniLM-384d; BGE-large-1024d narrows the gap (1/4 HB-significant). All results use HarmThoughts; generalization to multi-turn, multilingual, or encoded-prompt settings is untested. Probing results are correlational; causal intervention is needed to confirm mechanistic involvement.

